{"name":"Deep Cove","hex":"#051040","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"cove","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hollow in a rock; a cave or cavern. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A concave vault or archway, especially the arch of a ceiling."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To arch over; to build in a hollow concave form; to make in the form of a cove."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cove"}],"entries":[{"title":"Deep Cove, North Vancouver","blocks":[{"text":"Deep Cove refers to the community in the easternmost part of the District of North Vancouver, in British Columbia, Canada, and is also the geographic name of the small bay beside the town. It is affectionately referred to as \"The Cove\" by local residents. Located at the foot of Mount Seymour, Deep Cove faces due east, fronting onto Indian Arm, a branch of the Burrard Inlet. The area is the traditional territory of the Tsleil-Waututh and Squamish Nations. Deep Cove is 13 kilometres (8.1 mi) from downtown Vancouver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Deep_cove2560.jpg/330px-Deep_cove2560.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Cove%2C_North_Vancouver","wikidata":"Q5250114"},{"title":"Deep Cove (New Zealand)","blocks":[{"text":"Deep Cove is an arm of Doubtful Sound, a deep indentation in the southwest coast of New Zealand's South Island. Along with the Hall Arm, which lies to the southwest of Deep Cove, it forms one of the two most remote parts of the sound from the Tasman Sea, with its mouth being 32 kilometres (20 mi) from the mouth of Doubtful Sound. Elizabeth Island lies close to the junction of Deep Cove and the Hall Arm. Deep Cove by itself is about four kilometres long and is home to several waterfalls, including Helena Falls and Lady Alice Falls."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Deep_Cove_in_Doubtful_Sound_in_front_of_Wilmot_Pass.jpg/330px-Deep_Cove_in_Doubtful_Sound_in_front_of_Wilmot_Pass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_Cove_(New_Zealand)","wikidata":"Q5250112"},{"title":"Cove","blocks":[{"text":"A cove is a small bay or coastal inlet. They usually have narrow, restricted entrances, are often circular or oval, and are often situated within a larger bay. Small, narrow, sheltered bays, inlets, tidal creeks, or recesses in a coast are often considered coves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/McWay_cove_1.jpg/330px-McWay_cove_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cove","wikidata":"Q31615"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Deepcove8.jpg/500px-Deepcove8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Deepcove8.jpg/1280px-Deepcove8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Deepcove8.jpg/500px-Deepcove8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Deep Cove offers kayaking activities from the marina, among other sports.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeepcove8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Deep_Cove_Buoy.jpg/500px-Deep_Cove_Buoy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Deep_Cove_Buoy.jpg/1280px-Deep_Cove_Buoy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Deep_Cove_Buoy.jpg/500px-Deep_Cove_Buoy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Science buoy deployed in Deep Cove, New Zealand in 2016 to detect stratification and mixing.  The buoy was developed at the Scripps Institute of Oceanography.[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeep_Cove_Buoy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a8/Figure_lulworth_formation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/a/a8/Figure_lulworth_formation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Map showing two examples of how coves form. The rock types are those of Lulworth Cove. In example A, a river breaks through the resistant chalk back rock and limestone, leaving the weak clays to be rapidly eroded. In example B, the sea breaks through the limestone, perhaps by forming a cave, and then erodes the clay away.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFigure_lulworth_formation.png"}],"sounds":{"deep":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/En-us-deep.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/8a/En-us-deep.ogg/En-us-deep.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b3/En-au-deep.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/b3/En-au-deep.ogg/En-au-deep.ogg.mp3"}],"cove":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/EN-AU_ck1_cove.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/8/86/EN-AU_ck1_cove.ogg/EN-AU_ck1_cove.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Kayaking in Deep Cove (45495868232)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2845495868232%29.jpg/500px-Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2845495868232%29.jpg","ratio":1.7782901738205037,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_(45495868232).jpg","share":0.09625},{"title":"Kayaking in Deep Cove (31673692778)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2831673692778%29.jpg/500px-Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2831673692778%29.jpg","ratio":1.7777777777777777,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_(31673692778).jpg","share":0.09375},{"title":"Kayaking in Deep Cove (45495863012)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2845495863012%29.jpg/500px-Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2845495863012%29.jpg","ratio":1.7777777777777777,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_(45495863012).jpg","share":0.085625},{"title":"Kayaking in Deep Cove (45495864022)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2845495864022%29.jpg/500px-Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_%2845495864022%29.jpg","ratio":1.642618073633321,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kayaking_in_Deep_Cove_(45495864022).jpg","share":0.08375},{"title":"Kayaking in Deep Cove (30605134657)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0